1
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,674
How can someone trade the price of palm oil without ever owning the palm oil itself?

2
00:00:09,414 --> 00:00:14,572
The answer lies in one of the core tools of the commodity world: the futures contract.

3
00:00:16,472 --> 00:00:21,990
In this video, we look at what a futures contract is, why it exists, and who uses it.

4
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:31,216
Let's start with a real-world problem. Imagine a palm oil producer with a harvest to sell in three months.

5
00:00:31,556 --> 00:00:36,066
The producer is worried: what if crude palm oil prices fall before then?

6
00:00:36,406 --> 00:00:42,932
At the same time, a refinery that needs to buy palm oil has the opposite worry: what if prices rise?

7
00:00:43,272 --> 00:00:48,790
Both face the same thing: price risk. Neither knows what the price will be in three months.

8
00:00:50,690 --> 00:01:00,120
A futures contract is a standardised, legally binding agreement to buy or sell an asset at a price agreed today, for a specified date in the future.

9
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:07,610
It is traded on a regulated exchange, so the quantity, quality and delivery terms are the same for every participant.

10
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:15,604
The asset the contract is based on is called the underlying asset. For FCPO, the underlying asset is crude palm oil.

11
00:01:17,504 --> 00:01:23,766
Futures markets grew out of this need. Producers and buyers wanted a way to lock in prices ahead of time.

12
00:01:24,106 --> 00:01:33,512
So the journey looks like this: physical palm oil, a futures contract based on it, exposure to its price, and finally, hedging that exposure.

13
00:01:33,852 --> 00:01:39,682
The contract lets participants manage price risk today, without exchanging the physical goods today.

14
00:01:41,582 --> 00:01:46,092
It is important to separate the physical commodity from the futures contract.

15
00:01:46,432 --> 00:01:53,390
Buying physical palm oil means paying the full value, then storing, transporting and looking after the goods.

16
00:01:53,730 --> 00:02:01,816
A futures position is a contractual obligation linked to the price of the asset. Holding one does not automatically mean you own the palm oil.

17
00:02:02,156 --> 00:02:13,026
FCPO is a physically delivered contract, but many participants, especially retail traders, close their positions before delivery. We cover this in Video 05.

18
00:02:14,926 --> 00:02:19,796
Who uses futures? Broadly, two groups: hedgers and speculators.

19
00:02:20,136 --> 00:02:26,830
Hedgers, like producers and refineries, use futures to reduce the impact of price changes on their business.

20
00:02:27,170 --> 00:02:36,768
For example, the producer can sell futures today. If prices fall, the gain on the futures position can help offset the lower price received for the physical oil.

21
00:02:37,508 --> 00:02:45,954
Speculators have no physical business to protect. They take positions to seek profit from price movements, and they accept the risk of loss.

22
00:02:46,294 --> 00:02:51,380
Their activity adds liquidity, making it easier for hedgers to find a counterparty.

23
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,082
Futures exist on many types of assets.

24
00:02:56,422 --> 00:03:07,292
Agricultural commodities like crude palm oil, soybean and wheat. Energy, like crude oil. Metals, like gold. And financial futures, such as stock index futures.

25
00:03:07,632 --> 00:03:15,694
Each contract has its own specifications: size, tick value, trading hours and settlement method. They are not all the same.

26
00:03:17,594 --> 00:03:20,232
So how is this different from investing in stocks?

27
00:03:20,572 --> 00:03:25,970
A share represents ownership in a company, and you can usually hold it with no expiry date.

28
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:33,388
A futures contract is an agreement with an expiry month. It gives exposure to a price, not ownership of a company.

29
00:03:33,728 --> 00:03:41,862
Futures can be bought or sold first, and they are traded on margin. That creates leverage, which magnifies both gains and losses.

30
00:03:43,962 --> 00:03:51,064
A futures contract is a standardised agreement to buy or sell an asset at a price agreed today, for a future date.

31
00:03:51,404 --> 00:03:54,690
The underlying asset is what the contract is based on.

32
00:03:55,030 --> 00:04:00,236
Hedgers manage price risk, while speculators seek opportunity and accept risk.

33
00:04:00,576 --> 00:04:04,870
Holding a futures position does not mean you automatically own the commodity.

34
00:04:05,210 --> 00:04:09,912
Futures have expiry months and are traded on margin, which creates leverage.

35
00:04:10,252 --> 00:04:13,634
And every futures product has its own specifications.

36
00:04:14,934 --> 00:04:23,812
Futures are not simply about buying and selling contracts. They are a market instrument for managing risk and taking opportunities from price changes.

37
00:04:25,912 --> 00:04:29,678
This is only the foundation for understanding the futures market.

38
00:04:30,018 --> 00:04:33,016
Know the product you trade before taking any risk.

39
00:04:33,356 --> 00:04:36,426
Continue your learning with Capital Market Institute.

40
00:04:38,326 --> 00:04:43,988
The references for this video are shown on screen. Always check the latest official specifications.

41
00:04:46,888 --> 00:04:55,766
